  <h2>What a daily report is for</h2>
  <p>
    A construction daily report is the dated, same-day record of everything that happened on a job
    site: who was there, what got built, what arrived, who visited, and what went wrong. Most
    general contractors require one before they will sign off on a day&rsquo;s work. Even when
    nobody asks, it is the cheapest protection a subcontractor carries. When a delay claim lands,
    when a GC disputes a change order, or when an inspector asks what was on site last Tuesday, the
    daily reports answer the question &mdash; if they were written on the day and say something
    specific.
  </p>
  <p>
    This template is built around the eight sections our
    <a href="/blog/construction-daily-report-template/">daily report guide</a> walks through (identification,
    personnel, work performed, equipment, materials, visitors and inspections, safety, delays) and it
    fits on one page so it actually gets filled out.
  </p>

  <h2>Field-by-field notes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Report number and contract day.</strong> Number reports in sequence and note the contract
      day (day 79 of 171, say). A gap in the sequence is a gap in the record.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Weather, twice.</strong> AM and PM temperature and conditions, wind and precipitation, and
      a separate line for hours lost to weather and what was affected. Weather delays are only recoverable
      if you wrote down the times.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Crew table.</strong> One row per person or classification, with hours and the area or task.
      The total and headcount are what your timesheets, T&amp;M tickets and any labor claim will be reconciled
      against, so make them agree.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Equipment.</strong> Owned or rented, hours used, and idle time with the reason. Idle rented
      equipment is a real cost in a disruption claim &mdash; but only if you logged it the day it sat.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Materials delivered.</strong> Quantity, vendor and ticket number, where it was stored, and
      any shorts or damage. This is your chain of custody and the backup for billing stored materials
      on a <a href="/templates/aia-g702-g703/">pay application</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Work performed.</strong> Use locations that match the drawings &mdash; building, floor,
      room, gridline &mdash; and quantities. Record what was planned but could not be done, and why. &ldquo;Continued
      rough-in&rdquo; is not a record.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Delays, disruptions and issues.</strong> The most skipped section and the most valuable
      one. Areas not ready, missing RFI answers, other trades in your space, late deliveries caused by
      others. Cause, who, times, impact. Facts, not opinions.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Visitors, inspections, safety, photos.</strong> Who came and why; what was inspected and
      the result; the toolbox talk topic and attendance; incidents or near-misses (write &ldquo;None&rdquo;
      if none); and the photo file references so the pictures can be found later.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>A worked example</h2>
  <p>
    The preview shows the completed example that ships with the download. On Tuesday, August 18,
    2026 &mdash; report DR-056, contract day 79 &mdash; a mechanical subcontractor has six people on
    site for 48.0 hours: a foreman, two journeyman plumbers, an apprentice, and a sheet metal
    journeyman and apprentice. A 90-ton crane sets RTU-2 and RTU-3 on the roof between 7:00 and
    11:30, domestic water rough-in in Suites 210&ndash;214 is complete, and the corridor trunk duct
    is hung on gridlines C&ndash;F. Three deliveries are logged with ticket numbers and storage
    locations. The city rough plumbing inspection passes. And in the delays box, the foreman records
    that a roof drain conflicts with the RTU-3 curb and that RFI-016 went to the GC at 3:15 PM
    &mdash; the entry that will matter when that conflict becomes a
    <a href="/templates/change-order/">change order</a>. Twelve photos are referenced by file name.
    That is a day&rsquo;s worth of protection on one page.
  </p>

  <h2>Daily report vs. T&amp;M ticket vs. timesheet</h2>
  <p>
    Three field documents overlap and are often confused. The daily report covers the whole day for
    the whole site. A <a href="/templates/tm-ticket/">T&amp;M ticket</a> covers one piece of directed
    extra work &mdash; its hours, materials and equipment &mdash; signed by the GC the same day so it
    can be priced into a change order. A
    <a href="/templates/timesheet/">weekly timesheet</a> covers one person&rsquo;s hours for payroll and
    job costing. The crew table on the daily report should reconcile with both: the same people, the same
    hours. If the daily report says eight hours and the T&amp;M ticket says ten, the ticket will be questioned.
  </p>
  <p>
    A <a href="/templates/work-order/">work order</a> is different again: it authorizes a specific job
    before it starts. The daily report is what proves it happened.
  </p>

  <h2>Using the Excel workbook</h2>
  <p>
    The Daily Report sheet mirrors the PDF and totals crew hours and headcount as you type. The
    Weekly Summary sheet is a person-by-day grid for the week: enter the week-ending Sunday and the
    dates fill in, copy each person&rsquo;s hours from the daily reports, and it totals hours per
    person and per day, days worked, and headcount by day. A final row asks whether a daily report
    was filed each day &mdash; a &ldquo;No&rdquo; is a gap you want to know about before anyone else
    does. Every formula in the workbook has been checked with real numbers.
  </p>

  <h2>Common mistakes this form is built to prevent</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Writing a headcount instead of names, hours and areas.</li>
    <li>Summarizing (&ldquo;weather delay&rdquo;) instead of recording times and impact.</li>
    <li>Leaving the delays box empty because nothing was &ldquo;our fault&rdquo;.</li>
    <li>Filling in a week of reports on Friday from memory.</li>
    <li>Editorializing about the GC instead of stating what happened and when.</li>
    <li>Skipping days &mdash; a rained-out day with no work is still a report.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    For the habit side &mdash; who owns it, when it gets filed, how to make it stick with a crew
    &mdash; read
    <a href="/blog/construction-site-log-best-practices/">construction site log best practices</a>.
    When a day&rsquo;s issue turns into extra work, move to the
    <a href="/templates/tm-ticket/">T&amp;M ticket</a> and then the
    <a href="/templates/change-order/">change order</a>; the daily report is the paper trail
    underneath both.
  </p>
